{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nGerrit change 2169 (parent task 0754-cfc5-e451-4e9b) added the explicit, opt-in, read-only\n`rebar tracker-footprint` command. LLM review recorded four non-blocking hardening\nopportunities. This task addresses them without changing the command's opt-in, read-only,\nunscheduled, non-gating, threshold-free contract.\n\nFindings:\n1. Correctness/misuse: the mounted `pack` layer measures only the primary common Git object\n   database (`objects/pack/*.pack`). An alternates-backed shared clone (`git clone --shared`)\n   keeps its objects in the borrowed alternate object database, so the primary pack layer can\n   report near-zero. The report already labels the object database `shared`, but a reader can\n   still mistake the small `pack.logical_bytes` for an exclusive/complete whole-object-store\n   measurement.\n2. Portability: an interface assertion (`checkout_allocated > pack_logical * 4`) assumes each\n   one-byte file is charged a full filesystem block; this fails on block-suballocating /\n   tail-packing filesystems.\n3. The allocation-unavailable text rendering path has no direct test.\n4. The CLI bad-argument (argparse) exit-2 path has no direct test.\n\n## Decision (recorded)\n\nRetain **primary-only** pack scope rather than enumerating alternate object databases.\nEnumerating alternates would count borrowed, shared objects as this clone's local footprint\nand contradict the established whole-clone inode-dedup philosophy (shared bytes are not this\nclone's cost). Instead make the primary-only measurement **explicitly non-exclusive**: add a\n`complete` boolean to the `pack` layer — `true` when the measured `.pack` files are the\ncomplete object store backing this checkout, `false` when the checkout borrows objects from an\nunmeasured alternate object database (`\"alternates\"` present in `shared_reasons`). A linked\nworktree's pack is measured from the common dir and remains `complete: true` (shared across\nworktrees but complete). The `pack` definition string is updated to state this, and the text\nrenderer surfaces `complete` with a clarifying note when false.\n\n## Plan\n\n- footprint.py: `_pack_layer` gains `complete: bool`; `measure_tracker` computes\n  `complete = \"alternates\" not in reasons`. Update `DEFINITIONS[\"pack\"]` to describe the\n  primary-only, non-exclusive semantics.\n- schema: add required `complete` boolean to `pack_layer`.\n- tracker_footprint.py `_render_text`: render `complete=…` for the pack layer, with a\n  parenthetical when non-exclusive.\n- Tests (RED→GREEN):\n  - Unit: standalone and linked-worktree pack `complete: true`; alternates pack `complete:\n    false`.\n  - Unit: injected-`st_blocks` assertion proves allocation = Σ(st_blocks·512) deterministically\n    (replaces the filesystem-ratio assumption).\n  - Unit (new tests/unit/commands/test_tracker_footprint.py): `_render_text` renders the\n    allocation-unavailable label.\n  - Interface: replace the `* 4` ratio with portable assertions (allocation available, small\n    file count, pack complete/standalone, pack>0); add a bad-argument exit-2 CLI test.\n- docs/user-guide.md, docs/reuse-surface.md: document the pack `complete` field.\n- Preserve opt-in, read-only, unscheduled, non-gating, threshold-free behavior.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The `pack` layer carries a `complete` boolean; an alternates-backed mounted clone reports\n      `complete: false` so it cannot be mistaken for an exclusive whole-object-store pack\n      measurement; standalone and linked-worktree report `complete: true`. (unit + schema)\n- [ ] A real `git clone --shared` fixture proves the alternates ownership/dedup semantics\n      without double-counting shared objects. (unit)\n- [ ] Allocation-unavailable text rendering and CLI bad-argument exit 2 have direct tests.\n- [ ] Allocation assertions use injected/stat capability rather than a filesystem block policy.\n- [ ] The command stays opt-in, read-only, unscheduled, non-gating, and threshold-free.\n- [ ] make lint, make typecheck, and focused tests pass; generated help/reference stay green."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787561450282414001,"uuid":"b833295f-ce49-493a-85c7-5b8ee6963129"}